    sourceIds: ['nist-sematech-handbook', 'isa-95', 'iso-9001'],
    cards: [
      { id: 'cb081-rolled-yield', term: 'Rolled throughput yield and loss Pareto', kind: 'calculation', tool_scope: 'calculation', match: ['rolled throughput yield', 'first pass yield', '수율 손실', '직행률', 'scrap rate', 'rework'], source_ids: ['nist-sematech-handbook'], summary: 'First-pass yield of a step is the fraction of units that pass without rework or scrap. Rolled throughput yield multiplies first-pass yields across steps and shows how much good product emerges without any rework; it is often much lower than the final yield, which hides rework. Pareto the losses by cause and step. Rework counts as hidden capacity loss even when final yield looks high.' },
      { id: 'cb081-spc-signals', term: 'Control chart special-cause signals', kind: 'method', tool_scope: 'reasoning-only', match: ['control chart', 'spc', '관리도', '통계적 공정 관리', 'special cause', 'out of control'], source_ids: ['nist-sematech-handbook'], summary: 'Control limits come from the process\'s own variation, not the specification. Points outside limits, long runs on one side of the centre line, or trends indicate special causes worth investigating. Subgroups should be formed so variation within them is common-cause only. Do not adjust a stable process for each common-cause fluctuation; that tampering increases variation.' },
      { id: 'cb081-capability', term: 'Process capability with measurement caveat', kind: 'calculation', tool_scope: 'calculation', match: ['process capability', 'cpk', '공정 능력', '공정능력지수', 'specification limits', 'gauge r&r'], source_ids: ['nist-sematech-handbook'], summary: 'Capability compares the specification width with process spread: one index uses the full width, another the distance from the mean to the nearer limit, which captures off-centring. It is meaningful only for a stable process and roughly normal data; otherwise report it as indicative. Measurement error inflates observed spread, so check the measurement system first; a gauge that consumes much of the tolerance makes capability numbers unreliable.' },
      { id: 'cb081-plant-hierarchy', term: 'Plant hierarchy integration mapping', kind: 'method', tool_scope: 'workspace-artifact', match: ['mes', 'isa-95', '제조 실행 시스템', '공장 계층', 'erp integration', 'plc'], source_ids: ['isa-95'], summary: 'Separate business planning and logistics (orders, inventory), manufacturing operations (scheduling, dispatch, genealogy, quality records) and control (PLCs, machine control, safety systems). Integrations should exchange defined objects between levels rather than letting business systems write directly to control. Each data item needs a system of record. Changes touching the control level require plant change control and safety review.' },
      { id: 'cb081-corrective-action', term: 'Root cause and corrective action evidence', kind: 'checklist', tool_scope: 'reasoning-only', match: ['root cause', 'corrective action', '근본 원인', '시정 조치', 'capa', '8d'], source_ids: ['iso-9001', 'nist-sematech-handbook'], summary: 'A root cause claim needs evidence that the defect occurs with the cause present and stops when it is removed or controlled. Contain first (quarantine suspect lots), then correct, then verify effectiveness over enough production to see the defect rate change. Corrective actions that only retrain operators without changing the process rarely hold. Record which evidence supports each step.' },
      { id: 'cb081-safety-boundary', term: 'Plant safety and conformity boundary', kind: 'boundary', tool_scope: 'reasoning-only', match: ['machine safety', 'interlock', '기계 안전', '안전 인터록', 'certification', 'conformity'], source_ids: ['iso-9001', 'isa-95'], summary: 'Changes to interlocks, guarding, emergency stops, safety PLCs or process limits that protect people require qualified safety engineering review under the site\'s jurisdiction and standards. Quality-system certification and product conformity are decided by accredited bodies and the manufacturer\'s responsible persons. This role lists the review needed and never signs off operational safety.' },
    ],
